PIA HTTP CLIENT — THINGS YOU'LL NEED TO LEARN
================================================

1. STATE MANAGEMENT
-------------------
Learn:
- Zustand / Context
- Global state vs local component state
- Store actions
- Selectors
- Subscribing to only part of the state
- Updating nested state
- Derived state

You'll need it for:
- Requests
- Open FileNodes
- Active FileNode
- Collections
- Folders
- Environments
- Response state
- UI state


2. REQUEST / DOMAIN MODELING
----------------------------
Learn:
- TypeScript interfaces
- Type aliases
- Discriminated unions
- Optional fields
- Nested objects
- Entity IDs

You'll need to model:

Request
├── method
├── url
├── params
├── headers
├── auth
└── body

Body
├── none
├── JSON
├── text
├── form-data
├── URL encoded
└── binary


3. FILE / FOLDER TREE
---------------------
Learn:
- Tree data structures
- Parent/child relationships
- Recursive components
- IDs and references
- Normalized state

You'll need:

Collection
└── Folder
    ├── Folder
    │   └── Request
    └── Request

You'll eventually need:
- Create
- Rename
- Delete
- Move
- Reorder
- Drag & drop
- Nested folders


4. FILENODES / OPEN TABS
------------------------
Learn:
- Open/close state
- Active item state
- Arrays of IDs
- Tab management

Example:

fileNodes = ["r1", "r2", "r3"]

activeFileId = "r2"

You'll need:
- Open request
- Switch request
- Close request
- Close others
- Close all
- Reopen
- Prevent duplicate tabs


5. REQUEST EDITING
------------------
Learn:
- Controlled inputs
- Form state
- Immutable updates
- Dynamic lists

You'll need editors for:

URL
Method
Query Params
Path Params
Headers
Auth
Body

Example:

updateHeader(requestId, headerId, {
    value: "application/json"
})


6. HTTP FUNDAMENTALS
--------------------
Learn:
- HTTP methods
- Headers
- Status codes
- Query parameters
- Path parameters
- Request body
- Content-Type
- Cookies
- Redirects
- HTTPS
- CORS

Basically understand what you're actually building.


7. FETCH / HTTP CLIENT
----------------------
Learn:
- fetch()
- Request
- Response
- RequestInit
- AbortController
- Streaming
- Timeouts
- Error handling

Flow:

Request object
      ↓
Build HTTP request
      ↓
fetch()
      ↓
Response
      ↓
Response state
      ↓
Response UI


8. URL BUILDING
---------------
Learn:
- URL API
- URLSearchParams
- Encoding
- Path parameters

Example:

/users/:id

becomes:

/users/123

And:

?page=1&limit=20


9. VARIABLES / ENVIRONMENTS
---------------------------
Learn:
- Variable resolution
- Scope
- String interpolation
- Environment management

Example:

{{baseUrl}}/users/{{userId}}

Environment:

baseUrl = https://api.example.com
userId = 123

Before sending:

https://api.example.com/users/123


10. AUTHENTICATION
------------------
Learn:
- Basic Auth
- Bearer tokens
- API keys
- OAuth 2.0
- Access tokens
- Refresh tokens

Your request object might contain:

auth:
  type: "bearer"
  token: "{{token}}"


11. REQUEST EXECUTION PIPELINE
------------------------------
Learn how to transform:

EDITOR REQUEST
      ↓
Resolve variables
      ↓
Build URL
      ↓
Build headers
      ↓
Apply authentication
      ↓
Build body
      ↓
Send HTTP request
      ↓
Receive response


12. RESPONSE HANDLING
---------------------
Learn:
- Response headers
- Status
- Status text
- Response body
- JSON parsing
- Text responses
- Binary responses
- Large responses
- Error responses

You'll eventually have:

Response
├── status
├── headers
├── body
├── size
├── duration
└── timestamp


13. ASYNC STATE
--------------
Learn:
- Promises
- async/await
- Loading states
- Error states
- Race conditions

Example:

isSending = true

send request

isSending = false


14. ABORT / CANCEL REQUEST
--------------------------
Learn:
- AbortController

User clicks:

[Cancel]

Your request needs to stop.


15. REQUEST HISTORY
-------------------
Learn:
- History data modeling
- Persistence
- Timestamps
- Request snapshots

Example:

History
├── GET /users
├── POST /login
├── GET /repos
└── DELETE /users/10


16. PERSISTENCE
---------------
Learn:
- localStorage
- IndexedDB
- Serialization
- Deserialization
- Autosave

Your app should survive:

Browser refresh
      ↓
Requests still exist


17. IMPORT / EXPORT
-------------------
Learn:
- JSON serialization
- File APIs
- File input
- Blob
- Downloading files
- Schema/versioning

Example:

PIA Request
      ↓
JSON
      ↓
Export file

And:

JSON
      ↓
Import
      ↓
PIA Request


18. DATA MIGRATION
------------------
Eventually your request format changes.

v1:

{
    url: "...",
    headers: []
}

v2:

{
    url: "...",
    headers: [],
    variables: []
}

You'll need:
- Schema versions
- Migration functions


19. DRAG & DROP
--------------
For your collection tree:

Drag Request
     ↓
Drop into Folder

Learn:
- Drag events
- Drop targets
- Reordering
- Tree manipulation


20. KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
----------------------
Eventually:

Ctrl + N     → New request
Ctrl + S     → Save
Ctrl + W     → Close request
Ctrl + Enter → Send
Ctrl + P     → Search


21. SEARCH
----------
You'll eventually want:

Search requests
Search collections
Search headers
Search URLs

Learn:
- Filtering
- Debouncing
- Search indexing


22. CACHING / PERFORMANCE
------------------------
You'll eventually have:

100s / 1000s of requests

Learn:
- Zustand selectors
- Memoization
- React.memo
- Virtualization
- Avoiding unnecessary renders


23. ERROR HANDLING
------------------
Handle:

Network error
CORS error
Timeout
Invalid URL
Invalid JSON
DNS failure
401
403
404
500
Connection refused

Don't let one failed request crash the UI.


24. SECURITY
------------
VERY IMPORTANT for an API client.

Learn:
- Token storage
- Secrets
- XSS
- Sensitive data
- Environment variables
- OAuth security
- Avoiding accidental token logging

Especially because users may store:

API keys
Passwords
Bearer tokens
OAuth tokens


25. CORS / PROXY
----------------
Browser clients have CORS restrictions.

You may eventually need:

React app
    ↓
Your backend/proxy
    ↓
External API

Learn:
- CORS
- Reverse proxy
- Preflight requests
- OPTIONS
- Same-origin policy


26. BACKEND / DESKTOP ARCHITECTURE
----------------------------------
If your client needs to behave more like real Postman:

Browser
   ↓
Backend / Proxy
   ↓
API

Or eventually:

Desktop app
   ↓
Native HTTP layer

You'll need to understand why browser HTTP
and desktop HTTP clients are different.


27. DATABASE / FILESYSTEM
-------------------------
If you make it a serious desktop app:

Workspace
    ↓
SQLite / filesystem
    ↓
Collections
Folders
Requests
Environments
History


28. UNDO / REDO
--------------
Once users edit requests heavily:

Ctrl + Z
Ctrl + Shift + Z

You'll need:
- State snapshots
- History stack
- Command patterns / patches


29. CONCURRENCY / RACE CONDITIONS
---------------------------------
Example:

Request A starts
Request B starts
Request B finishes
Request A finishes

Your UI must not accidentally display
A's result as B's result.

Learn:
- Request IDs
- AbortController
- Async state management


30. TESTING
-----------
Eventually test:

Request creation
Header updates
URL updates
Folder creation
Moving requests
Sending requests
Variable resolution
Auth
Import/export

Learn:
- Unit testing
- Integration testing
- React testing


31. TYPESCRIPT ADVANCED
----------------------
You'll naturally hit:

- Generics
- Discriminated unions
- Utility types
- Partial<T>
- Pick<T>
- Omit<T>
- Record<K,V>
- Type guards
- Type narrowing


32. ARCHITECTURE
----------------
Eventually you'll need to separate:

UI
 ↓
State
 ↓
Domain logic
 ↓
HTTP execution
 ↓
Persistence

Don't put everything inside React components.


================================================
THE FIRST THINGS YOU SHOULD LEARN
================================================

Don't learn all 32 now.

Your immediate order should be:

1. Zustand / state management
2. TypeScript data modeling
3. FileNode + Request relationship
4. Tree / folder structure
5. Controlled inputs
6. HTTP fundamentals
7. fetch + async state
8. Request → fetch transformation
9. Response state
10. Persistence

Then:

11. Variables / environments
12. Auth
13. Import / export
14. Drag & drop
15. History
16. Caching / performance
17. CORS / proxy
18. Security
19. Testing


